<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'

import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Exchange from '#src/lib/Exchange.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import SiteTree from '#src/lib/SiteTree.abide'
</script>

<Doc
    seeAlso={[
        '/pages/files',
        '/pages/pages',
        '/handlers/rpc',
        '/middleware/middleware',
    ]}
>
    <Section title="Give each feature of the app one directory">
        <p>The CRM keeps a directory per thing a rep works on: contacts, companies, deals, the
            pipeline. A feature's page, the handlers it calls, its components and its tests sit
            together, so a feature is one directory to read and one to delete.</p>
        <Code code={`
            src/
              layout.abide     the document around every page
              index.abide      /
              contacts/        /contacts, and what its page calls
              companies/       /companies, and a page per company
              deals/
              pipeline/
              account/
              lib/             shared by every feature, no address
              public/          files answered as they are
        `} />
        <p>Each file's path is its address, and its name says what it is.
            <a href={url('/pages/files')}>Files</a> lists every name abide reads.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Start a feature with its page, its handlers and a lib">
        <p><code>index.abide</code> answers at the directory's address. Each
            <code>.server.ts</code> exporting an <code>rpc</code> answers at its own name below it,
            and <code>lib/</code> holds
            what the page mounts but nobody visits. The CRM's <code>contacts</code>, read as this
            page rendered:</p>
        <SiteTree directory="contacts" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Keep a page and the handlers it calls in one directory">
        <p>A component imports its handler by a relative path and calls it like a function. On the
            server the call runs the handler, and in the browser it fetches the address the file
            answers at. <a href={url('/handlers/rpc')}><code>rpc</code></a> declares one.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { reactive } from '@abide/abide'
            import listContacts from '../listContacts.server.ts'

            const contacts = reactive(() => listContacts())
            </script>
        `} />
        <p>The same handler, asked at its address when this page loaded:</p>
        <Exchange path="/contacts/listContacts" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Answer every company with one file">
        <p>A segment in brackets is a value the page reads from
            <a href={url('/pages/route')}><code>route.params</code></a>. A matcher named after
            <code>=</code> turns away an id that cannot be a company before the page runs.
            <a href={url('/pages/pages')}>Pages</a> has every kind of segment.</p>
        <SiteTree directory="companies" />
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`
            // A company's id: lowercase words and digits, joined by hyphens.
            export const SLUG = /[a-z0-9-]+/
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Wrap one corner of the site in a layout of its own">
        <p>A <code>layout.abide</code> wraps every page at its directory and below, inside the
            layouts above it. The CRM's admin page sits in <code>account/admin</code>, so the
            admins-only rung in that layout reaches it alone. The <code>error.abide</code> beside
            it paints the refusal.</p>
        <SiteTree directory="account/admin" />
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script server>
            import { middleware } from '@abide/abide/server'
            import { adminsOnly } from '../lib/adminsOnly.ts'

            middleware(adminsOnly)
            </script>

            <slot />
        `} />
        <p>A rung is a <a href={url('/middleware/middleware')}><code>middleware</code></a>, and a
            layout runs it for everything under its directory.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Guard the handlers under a directory with its layout">
        <p>A layout's rung runs for the handlers below it as well as the pages. The CRM's
            <code>pipeline/archive</code> has no page at all: its layout makes every handler under
            it read-only, so a write there is refused.</p>
        <SiteTree directory="pipeline/archive" />
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script server>
            import { middleware, readOnly } from '@abide/abide/server'

            middleware(readOnly({ message: 'Last year’s pipeline is archived.' }))
            </script>

            <slot />
        `} />
        <Exchange method="POST" path="/pipeline/archive/reopenDeal" />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Keep the records under lib, out of every browser's reach">
        <p>Anything under a <code>lib</code> directory is imported and never visited. A
            <code>.server.ts</code> file there runs on the server alone, and a page importing one
            fails the build. The CRM's companies live in
            <code>companies/lib/COMPANIES.server.ts</code>, and a handler reads them.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { refuse } from '@abide/abide'
            import { rpc } from '@abide/abide/server'
            import { COMPANIES } from './lib/COMPANIES.server.ts'

            export default rpc(async ({ id }: { id: string }) => {
                const company = COMPANIES.find((one) => one.id === id)
                if (company === undefined) return refuse.notFound()
                return company
            })
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Serve the app's icon as a file from public">
        <p>A file under <code>public</code> answers at its path below that directory, as
            written, with no handler in front of it.</p>
        <Code code={`
            src/public/icon.png    /icon.png
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Read the whole CRM as the addresses it answers at">
        <p>Every file of the CRM outside a <code>lib</code>, read as this page rendered. Each page
            with no dynamic segment links to where it answers.</p>
        <SiteTree directory="" />
    </Section>
</Doc>
